---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Valuation Score Methodology | Price-vs-Trend Read-out">
  <section class="py-16 bg-[#161B24]">
    <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-center mb-4">How the Valuation Score Works</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-[#CBD5E1] text-center mb-12 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
        A simple read of where a stock trades relative to its own recent price trend — useful context,
        <strong class="text-white">not</strong> a buy signal.
      </p>

      <!-- Honest framing up front -->
      <div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-slate-800/40 to-slate-700/20 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-12 border border-amber-500/40">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
          <span class="text-3xl">📐</span>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-amber-300">What this is — and what it isn't</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="text-[#CBD5E1]">
          Despite the name, the Valuation Score is a <strong class="text-white">technical, price-based</strong>
          read-out — it measures how far a stock trades above or below its own moving averages and where it sits
          in its 52-week range. It is <strong class="text-white">not</strong> a fundamental valuation (that's
          earnings, cash flow, multiples — see Compass), and <strong class="text-white">it does not predict
          returns.</strong> When we tested it honestly (below), "trading below the average" did <em>not</em>
          reliably lead to outperformance. Treat it as a descriptive snapshot of price position — context to pair
          with fundamentals and analyst targets, never a signal on its own.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- What It Measures -->
      <div class="bg-[#080A0F] rounded-2xl p-8 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-6">What it measures</h2>
        <p class="text-[#CBD5E1] mb-6">
          Three price-based inputs, combined into a 0–100 score (higher = trading lower relative to its own
          recent prices):
        </p>

        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
          <div class="bg-[#161B24] rounded-xl p-6 border border-[rgba(255,255,255,0.06)]">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
              <span class="text-2xl">📊</span>
              <h3 class="font-semibold">Price vs 200-day average</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="text-[#CBD5E1] text-sm">
              How far is the price from its 200-day moving average? The main long-term trend reference.
            </p>
            <div class="mt-3 text-[#22D3EE] font-semibold">Weight: 50%</div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-[#161B24] rounded-xl p-6 border border-[rgba(255,255,255,0.06)]">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
              <span class="text-2xl">📈</span>
              <h3 class="font-semibold">Price vs 50-day average</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="text-[#CBD5E1] text-sm">
              The shorter-term trend — how the price sits versus the last ~10 weeks.
            </p>
            <div class="mt-3 text-[#22D3EE] font-semibold">Weight: 25%</div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-[#161B24] rounded-xl p-6 border border-[rgba(255,255,255,0.06)]">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
              <span class="text-2xl">📉</span>
              <h3 class="font-semibold">52-week position</h3>
            </div>
            <p class="text-[#CBD5E1] text-sm">
              Where the price sits between its 52-week low and high — near the low or near the high.
            </p>
            <div class="mt-3 text-[#22D3EE] font-semibold">Weight: 25%</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- What we found when we tested it (honest) -->
      <div class="bg-[#161B24] rounded-2xl p-8 mb-12 border border-amber-500/30">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 text-amber-300">What we found when we tested it — honestly</h2>
        <p class="text-[#CBD5E1] mb-4">
          We re-ran this signal through a survivorship-clean backtest (out-of-sample, 2020 onward, failed
          companies kept in). The popular idea that "stocks below their moving averages bounce back" did
          <strong class="text-white">not</strong> hold up:
        </p>
        <ul class="space-y-3 text-[#CBD5E1]">
          <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-amber-400">•</span>
            <span>Over the recent period, the "below-average" group <strong class="text-white">underperformed</strong>
            the "above-average" group on a like-for-like basis — the opposite of a bounce. A falling stock often
            keeps falling.</span></li>
          <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-amber-400">•</span>
            <span>The result <strong class="text-white">flips with the market regime</strong> — it helped sharply
            coming off the 2009 and 2020 crash bottoms, but hurt in normal and trending markets. There's no stable,
            dependable edge either way.</span></li>
          <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-amber-400">•</span>
            <span>Combining it with quality (Compass) did <strong class="text-white">not</strong> improve a
            quality screen — it slightly diluted it.</span></li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-[#CBD5E1] text-sm mt-4">
          So we make <strong class="text-white">no performance claim</strong> for this score and we've removed the
          old "expected return" figures that earlier versions of this page showed. It stays on the site as an
          honest <em>descriptive</em> read of price position — nothing more. <em>(For signals that did hold up in
          testing, see the <a href="/catalyst-methodology" class="text-[#22D3EE] hover:underline">Catalyst Signal</a>
          and <a href="/golden-methodology" class="text-amber-300 hover:underline">Golden Stocks</a>.)</em>
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- How to read it -->
      <div class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-6">How to read the score</h2>
        <p class="text-[#CBD5E1] mb-6">These are <strong>descriptions of price position</strong>, not buy/sell calls:</p>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mb-8">
          <div class="bg-[#080A0F] rounded-xl p-6 border-l-4 border-emerald-500">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-emerald-400 mb-2">70+ · Below its averages</h3>
            <p class="text-[#CBD5E1] text-sm">
              Trading below its recent trend. That can mean a pullback — or a business in decline. The score can't
              tell which, so check the fundamentals and why it's down.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-[#080A0F] rounded-xl p-6 border-l-4 border-yellow-500">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-yellow-400 mb-2">40–70 · Around its averages</h3>
            <p class="text-[#CBD5E1] text-sm">
              Trading near its recent trend — no strong price-position signal either way.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-[#080A0F] rounded-xl p-6 border-l-4 border-red-500">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-red-400 mb-2">&lt;40 · Above its averages</h3>
            <p class="text-[#CBD5E1] text-sm">
              Trading above its recent trend — could be strong momentum, or extended. Again, the score is
              descriptive; it isn't predicting a drop.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Using with analyst price targets -->
      <div class="bg-[#161B24] rounded-2xl p-8 mb-12 border border-[rgba(255,255,255,0.1)]">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Using it with analyst price targets</h2>
        <p class="text-[#CBD5E1]">
          The most useful role for a price-position read is <strong class="text-white">context for the analyst
          price targets</strong> shown on each stock page. If a stock is trading well below its trend <em>and</em>
          analysts see upside, the two views agree it's beaten down — but agreement isn't a guarantee, and a low
          price can reflect real problems. If it's well above its trend while analysts still see big upside, that's
          worth a closer look before chasing it. Use it to frame questions, not to make the decision.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Limitations -->
      <div class="bg-[#080A0F] rounded-2xl p-8 mb-12 border border-red-500/20">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 text-red-400">Limitations, plainly</h2>
        <ul class="space-y-3 list-disc list-outside ml-6 text-[#CBD5E1]">
          <li><strong>Not a predictor.</strong> Our own testing found it doesn't reliably forecast returns. It
            describes where the price is, not where it's going.</li>
          <li><strong>"Below average" ≠ cheap.</strong> A stock below its SMA200 may be a bargain or may be falling
            for good reasons (collapsing earnings, structural decline). The score can't distinguish them.</li>
          <li><strong>Regime-dependent.</strong> Whatever tendency it has flips between crash-rebound and normal
            markets, so it's unreliable as a rule.</li>
          <li><strong>Pair it, don't follow it.</strong> Use it only alongside fundamentals (Compass) and the
            catalyst/analyst picture — never as a standalone buy or sell signal.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- Links to Other Methodologies -->
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mb-12">
        <div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-cyan-900/20 to-blue-900/20 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-cyan-500/20">
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold mb-2 text-cyan-300">Compass Score</h3>
          <p class="text-[#CBD5E1] text-sm mb-4">Company quality — profitability, cash flow, stability.</p>
          <a href="/how-it-works" class="text-cyan-400 hover:text-cyan-300 font-semibold">How Compass works →</a>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-[#22D3EE]/10 to-blue-900/20 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-[#22D3EE]/30">
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold mb-2 text-[#22D3EE]">Catalyst Signal</h3>
          <p class="text-[#CBD5E1] text-sm mb-4">Recent earnings beats + analyst-upgrade momentum.</p>
          <a href="/catalyst-methodology" class="text-[#22D3EE] hover:underline font-semibold">How Catalyst works →</a>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-amber-900/20 to-yellow-900/20 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-amber-500/20">
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold mb-2 text-amber-300">Golden Stocks</h3>
          <p class="text-[#CBD5E1] text-sm mb-4">Top-decile quality + top-decile catalyst.</p>
          <a href="/golden-methodology" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300 font-semibold">About Golden Stocks →</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="text-center">
        <a href="/tool" class="inline-block bg-[#22D3EE] text-[#0C0F14] px-8 py-4 rounded-lg font-semibold text-lg hover:opacity-90 transition">
          Try the Stock Screener
        </a>
        <p class="text-xs text-[#64748B] mt-6 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
          Stockbrowse is for research and education, not financial advice. The Valuation Score is a descriptive
          technical indicator with no performance claim; past price patterns don't predict future returns.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
